public class FollowUpTaskService {

    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate so the same opportunity isn't processed twice
        Set<Id> uniqueIds = new Set<Id>(opportunityIds);

        // Single SOQL – one query regardless of list size
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :uniqueIds]
        );

        Date activityDate = Date.today().addDays(7);

        for (Id oppId : uniqueIds) {
            Opportunity opp = oppMap.get(oppId);

            // Id was passed in but no matching Opportunity exists
            if (opp == null) {
                failedIds.add(oppId);
                continue;
            }

            try {
                Task task = new Task(
                    WhatId       = opp.Id,
                    OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                    Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                    ActivityDate = activityDate,
                    Status       = 'Not Started',
                    Priority     = 'Normal'
                );
                insert task;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Validation rule or other DmlException on this one task –
                // record the failure and keep going
                failedIds.add(oppId);
            }
        }

        return failedIds;
    }
}
```

---

**What I would do differently, and why**

The code above is exactly what was asked for – a straight loop with an individual `insert` inside a `try/catch`. It is correct, and it will work for small batches. But it has a hard, non-negotiable problem at the volume you described (150–250 opportunities per call):

**1. Governor-limit breach on DML statements**
Each `insert task;` is one DML statement. The platform limit is **150 DML statements per synchronous transaction**. At 200 opportunities you are at 200 statements; at 250 you are at 250. The call will throw `LimitExceeded: DML statements` part-way through the loop, and because it is a synchronous method the entire transaction rolls back – every task that *was* successfully inserted gets undone. The "one bad task shouldn't stop the others" guarantee is broken not by a validation rule but by the platform itself.

**2. The fix: `Database.saveRecords`**
Replace the loop of individual inserts with a single bulk call:

```apex
// Build the full list of tasks in memory (no DML)
List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
Map<Id, Id> taskToOppId = new Map<Id, Id>();   // Task Id → Opp Id (set after save)

for (Id oppId : uniqueIds) {
    Opportunity opp = oppMap.get(oppId);
    if (opp == null) {
        failedIds.add(oppId);
        continue;
    }
    Task task = new Task(
        WhatId       = opp.Id,
        OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
        Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
        ActivityDate = activityDate,
        Status       = 'Not Started',
        Priority     = 'Normal'
    );
    tasks.add(task);
}

// ONE DML statement, no matter how many tasks
List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.saveRecords(tasks);

// Walk the results – a validation-rule rejection shows up here,
// not as an exception
for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
    if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
        // map back to the opportunity
        // (you can store the mapping in a parallel list or use the
        //  Task's WhatId from the pre-insert object)
    }
}
```

Why this is strictly better:

| Concern | Loop of `insert` | `Database.saveRecords` |
|---|---|---|
| DML statements used | *N* (150–250) → **exceeds limit** | **1** |
| One record rejected by a validation rule | Throws `DmlException`; caught by `try/catch` | `SaveResult.isSuccess() == false`; others still commit |
| Rollback on failure | Only the failed insert rolls back (because it threw) | Per-record; the rest commit normally |
| Readability at 250 records | 250 lines of identical `try/catch` | One call, one result loop |
| `try/catch` needed? | Yes | No – you inspect `SaveResult` |

The only subtlety is mapping each `SaveResult` back to its opportunity Id. Because `saveRecords` does not return the original object references, you keep a parallel `List<Id> taskOppIds` (same order as `tasks`) so you can look up `taskOppIds[i]` when `results[i]` fails.

**3. Minor notes on the code as written**

* I de-duplicate the input with a `Set` so a repeated Id doesn't produce two tasks for the same opportunity.
* I query only the three fields I need (`Id`, `Name`, `OwnerId`) rather than `SELECT *`.
* I check for `opp == null` (Id was passed but the record doesn't exist or the caller has no read access) and treat it as a failure rather than letting the `insert` throw on a null `WhatId`.
* `Status = 'Not Started'` and `Priority = 'Normal'` are the default picklist values on `Task`, so they would work even if omitted, but I include them explicitly so the intent is visible and the code survives a future change to the defaults.

**Bottom line:** the loop-and-`try/catch` pattern is the right shape for *learning* or for very small lists (< 100). For the 150–250 volume sales ops is passing in a single call, `Database.saveRecords` is the production-safe choice. It gives you the exact same "one failure doesn't stop the rest" semantics you need, without ever touching the DML governor limit.

