public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * For each lead (same order/size) returns the Id of the first contact whose
     * email matches the lead's email (case-insensitive, trimmed), or null.
     * No SOQL, no DML – purely in-memory.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> results = new List<Id>(leads.size());

        // Pre-normalise every contact email into a map.
        // "First in contacts order" is guaranteed because we skip keys already present.
        Map<String, Id> emailToContactId = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                String key = normalize(c.Email);
                if (key != null && !emailToContactId.containsKey(key)) {
                    emailToContactId.put(key, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // One O(1) lookup per lead.
        for (Lead l : leads) {
            String key = normalize(l.Email);
            results.add(key == null ? null : emailToContactId.get(key));
        }

        return results;
    }

    /**
     * Trim + lower-case a raw email. Returns null for null / blank values so
     * that blank emails can never match.
     */
    private static String normalize(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return null;
        }
        String trimmed = email.trim();
        if (trimmed == '') {
            return null;
        }
        return trimmed.toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

**Why I'd do it differently from a nested loop**

The nested loop you described works and is correct, but it's O(n × m) — with the stated ceiling of 3 000 × 3 000 that's up to **9 million** `trim()` / `toLowerCase()` calls inside a single synchronous transaction. Each string allocation adds pressure on the 10-second CPU governor, and the constant is non-trivial in Apex because every string method call goes through the managed runtime.

A `Map<String, Id>` built once over the contacts makes the whole method **O(n + m)**:

| Step | Cost |
|---|---|
| Build map (contacts) | 3 000 normalise + put |
| Look up (leads) | 3 000 normalise + get |
| **Total string ops** | **≈ 6 000** (vs. ≈ 9 000 000) |

It's still very readable (two short loops), it preserves "first in `contacts` order" naturally (skip keys already in the map), and it keeps head-room for the rest of the import transaction (field mapping, validation, DML). The only trade-off is a small amount of heap for the map (a few thousand entries), which is negligible.

If the lists were truly tiny (dozens) the nested loop would be perfectly fine and marginally less code, but at the 3 000 scale the map is strictly better with no readability cost.

## Explanation of design decisions

The requirement to "enqueue one job per case" so that a single failure can't affect the others conflicts with the platform limit of **50 enqueued Queueable jobs per transaction**. Agents can close 200+ cases in one bulk save, so a naïve `for … System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))` loop would throw `LimitExceeded: Too many queueable jobs enqueued`.

To stay within the limit while preserving the "one job per case" isolation guarantee, I use a **dispatcher/worker pattern** inside the single `CaseSurveyJob` class:

| Role | Trigger | Job |
|---|---|---|
| **Dispatcher** | Enqueues `CaseSurveyJob(List<Id>)` in chunks of 50 (≤ 50 total enqueues in the trigger transaction). | Receives a `List<Id>` and enqueues one `CaseSurveyJob(Id)` worker per case (≤ 50 enqueues in that transaction). |
| **Worker** | — | Receives a single `Id`, queries the Case, and inserts the Task. |

This keeps every case in its **own** Apex job (full isolation), handles the 200-case nightly cleanup (4 dispatchers × 50 workers), and never exceeds the per-transaction enqueue limit.

Other notes:

* The worker re-queries the Case by Id rather than passing all fields from the trigger, because the job runs asynchronously and the record state should be authoritative at execution time.
* A defensive null-check on `ContactId` inside the worker guards against the (rare) race where a contact is removed between trigger fire and job execution.
* No `@isTest` or other metadata is needed; the `-meta.xml` files are generated by the project.

