     * @return Number of emails that were sent successfully.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // One SOQL for the whole batch – keeps us well under the 100-query limit.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            Opportunity opp = oppMap.get(oppId);
            if (opp == null || opp.OwnerId == null) {
                continue; // not found or unowned – skip silently
            }

            try {
                Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
                email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
                email.setSaveAsActivity(false);
                email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
                email.setPlainTextBody(
                    'Hello,\n\n'
                    + 'Your opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" has a close date of '
                    + opp.CloseDate
                    + '. Please follow up with the customer to confirm the renewal.\n\n'
                    + 'Thanks'
                );

                // One sendEmail call per opportunity so a single failure
                // does not cascade to the rest.
                List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> results =
                    Messaging.sendEmail(new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>{ email });

                if (results != null && !results.isEmpty() && results[0].isSuccess()) {
                    sentCount++;
                } else {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                        'sendEmail returned non-success for opportunity ' + oppId
                        + (results != null && !results.isEmpty()
                            ? ' – message: ' + results[0].getMessage()
                            : ''));
                }
            } catch (Messaging.EmailSendException e) {
                // Log and continue – the next opportunity should still be sent.
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'Email send failed for opportunity ' + oppId + ': ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

## Why I'm changing your approach

Your instinct—"one query, done, no queries in loops"—is exactly right, and I'm keeping it. But the specific query you sketched, `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]` with no `WHERE` clause, will pull all 2.3 million contacts into the transaction. That blows well past the 50 000-row SOQL limit for a synchronous trigger and would try to allocate tens of megabytes of heap for a `Map<Id, Contact>`. Even in a batch of 200 accounts the org still has 2.3 M contacts; an unbounded query doesn't get smaller.

The fix is trivial: add `WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds`. It's still **one** SOQL statement, still no loops, but the result set is bounded to the contacts of the 200 (or fewer) accounts actually being updated in that transaction—typically a few hundred to a few thousand rows. Same pattern, just scoped.

Everything else stays as you described: one DML `update` on the filtered list, no per-record queries, no `Database` wrapper needed (standard DML is fine here), and the trigger stays thin.

